<Guide
  path="/guide/parts/"
  title="The six parts of a repair record"
  description="What was reported, what the contractor says, checks and evidence, the resident's answer, who closed it and why, and later additions."
>
  <p class="eyebrow">The guide</p>
  <h1>The six parts of a repair record</h1>
  <p class="lead">A repair completion report has six parts. Each answers one question; together they make a report that someone outside your system can read.</p>

  <h2 id="reported">1. What was reported</h2>
  <p>
    Where (the building and unit, or the common area), when, who reported it, and <strong>the symptom in the
    reporter's own words</strong>: "water is dripping from the AC in the living room". If the equipment is known,
    add its tag or a plain label.
  </p>
  <p>
    Keep the symptom apart from the diagnosis. "Blocked condensate drain" is the contractor's view of the cause and
    belongs in their statement. If the fault comes back, the original symptom is what you compare against.
  </p>

  <h2 id="contractor">2. What the contractor says</h2>
  <p>
    One entry per visit or report of work: who made the statement, when, what they did, the cause if they know it,
    and the outcome they report:
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Outcome</th><th>Use it when</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Resolved</strong></td><td>The contractor believes the fault is fixed.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Temporary fix</strong></td><td>The symptom is stopped, but the real repair is still to come.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Partly done</strong></td><td>Some of the work is done; the rest needs another visit or parts.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Not done</strong></td><td>The contractor came but could not do the work.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>No access</strong></td><td>Nobody let the contractor in.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>This is a statement, not a verdict. The report shows it as "Statement by …", with a name and a time.</p>

  <h2 id="checks">3. Checks and evidence</h2>
  <p>
    A check is anything someone did to confirm the work: a test run, a measurement, a look, a review of a photo.
    For each, write <strong>who checked, how, what they found, and what the check did not cover</strong>. "Ran the
    unit for 15 minutes, no leak while watching" and "did not inspect the unit" are both useful facts.
  </p>
  <p>
    Evidence is what backs a statement or a check: a photo, a measurement, a test log, a certificate. Note what it
    shows and who made it. Photos are useful, but never required: for many jobs a photo proves very little, and a
    form that refuses a report without one only teaches people to take meaningless photos.
  </p>
  <p>If nobody checked, leave it empty. The report will say "no check is recorded", which is honest.</p>

  <h2 id="resident">4. The resident's answer</h2>
  <p>Is the problem the resident reported solved, in their view? There are six possible answers, and they are not interchangeable:</p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Answer</th><th>Means</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Resolved</strong></td><td>The resident says it is solved. Quote their words.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Not resolved</strong></td><td>The resident says it is not. The record should not be closed as done.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Not asked yet</strong></td><td>Nobody has asked.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Asked, waiting</strong></td><td>The question was sent; there is no answer yet.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>No reply</strong></td><td>The question was sent and nothing came back. This stays "no reply"; it is never turned into a yes.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Not applicable</strong></td><td>There is no resident to ask: a lobby light, a car park barrier, a plant room.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>Record when the resident was asked and, if they answered, when. The date of the question is what makes "no reply" meaningful.</p>

  <h2 id="closure">5. Who closed it, and why</h2>
  <p>
    Closing a record is a decision someone takes. Write who took it, when, and the reason: confirmed by the
    resident; work reported done with no reply; an administrative reason; a duplicate; cancelled. If anything is
    still open, list it with an owner, a next step and a date, and think twice about closing.
  </p>
  <p>
    Closing a job without a confirmation is sometimes the right call. A record that says "closed: work reported done,
    asked the resident on 11 Oct, no reply after four days" is a perfectly good record. A record that says "resolved"
    when nobody knows is not.
  </p>

  <h2 id="history">6. Later additions</h2>
  <p>
    A late reply, a revisit, a correction: add it as a new entry with a time and an author. If a closed record has to
    be reopened, the earlier closure is kept in the history as it was, and the new visit becomes another statement.
    Nothing earlier is overwritten, so anyone can follow what happened in order.
  </p>
</Guide>
